# Polymorphism in C++: Payment Syste
This program demonstrates **runtime polymorphism** using a `virtual` function. One function call behaves differently depending on the actual type of the object passed in.
 Payment (base)
CashPayment    MobileMoneyPayment

# The Classes

**`Payment` (base class)**
- Holds `amount`, the payment value.
- Declares `makePayment()` as `virtual`, which allows derived classes to replace its behavior.

**`CashPayment` and `MobileMoneyPayment` (derived classes)**
- Each inherits publicly from `Payment`.
- Each **overrides** `makePayment()` with its own message.
- The `override` keyword makes the compiler check that the function really overrides a virtual one, catching typos and signature mistakes.

## The Key Function
void processPayment(Payment& p)
{
    p.makePayment();
}

- It takes a **reference to the base class**, so it accepts any `Payment` or any class derived from it.
- Because `makePayment()` is virtual, C++ decides **at runtime** which version to call, based on the real object, not the reference type.
- Without `virtual`, it would always call `Payment::makePayment()` and print "Processing payment".

## The main() Function
1. Creates a `CashPayment` and a `MobileMoneyPayment` object.
2. Sets their amounts to 50000 and 100000.
3. Passes each one to `processPayment()`.

#Output
PAYMENT SYSTEM
Cash payment made: UGX 50000
Mobile money payment made: UGX 100000

# Key Concepts i learnt with this:

 Concept and Where it appears 
 Inheritance - Both payment classes derive from `Payment` |
 Virtual function -`virtual void makePayment()` |
 Overriding - `makePayment() override` in each derived class |
 Polymorphism -`processPayment(Payment& p)` works with any payment type |

